  "title": "Chapter 5 – Pathology",
  "order": 5,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "lyb7358",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Cellular adaptations include hypertrophy, hyperplasia, atrophy, and metaplasia as responses to persistent stress.",
        "Cell death primarily occurs via two distinct mechanisms: Necrosis (pathological, uncontrolled) and Apoptosis (programmed, controlled).",
        "Acute inflammation is characterized by vascular changes, leukocyte recruitment, and five cardinal signs.",
        "Neoplasia represents abnormal, uncoordinated cellular proliferation categorized into benign or malignant tumors.",
        "Immune disorders encompass hypersensitivity reactions, autoimmune pathologies, and immunodeficiency states."
      ],
      "id": "zya2hnw"
    },
    {
