    {
      "id": "j6al2zi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Blood Disorders"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Metaplasia is the reversible replacement of one adult cell type by another adult cell type in response to chronic irritation.",
        "Apoptosis does not trigger an inflammatory response, whereas Necrosis typically causes surrounding inflammation.",
        "The five cardinal signs of acute inflammation are Rubor (redness), Calor (heat), Tumor (swelling), Dolor (pain), and Functio Laesa (loss of function).",
        "Malignancy is definitively differentiated from benign neoplasms by local invasion, rapid cellular proliferation, and metastasis."
      ],
      "id": "ff30n3o"
